query, and fragment are replaced by \verb{/robots.txt}. Within one call each
distinct grouping key is fetched exactly once and every fetch-eligible row
sharing that key reuses the same source body; source IDs are assigned
\code{robots_1}, \code{robots_2}, and so on in first-occurrence order. There is no
persistent, cross-call, or hidden HTTP cache.

The original input URL string is passed to the matcher unchanged. R does not
clean, decode, canonicalize, or reserialize it: percent escapes, Unicode,
path case, query strings, and fragments all reach the matcher as supplied.
\code{rurl} is used only to construct the fetch-origin grouping key, never to
transform the matcher input. The upstream matcher requires an appropriately
encoded, \verb{\%}-escaped full URL (per RFC 3986); callers are responsible for
supplying it in that form.

\code{url} may have length zero (an empty result is returned). \code{user_agent} must
be a character vector of length one or \code{length(url)}: a scalar user agent
expands across every URL, and no other recycling is allowed. A wrong R type,
